    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter July 2nd with a focus on maximizing value against favorable matchups. Our strongest play of the day is **Junior Caminero**, boasting a 73.67 score. Batting 3rd against Kolek, **Junior Caminero** benefits from an optimal lineup slot and a +8% SP skill boost, making him our primary engine for counting stats. Joining him in the top tier are **Trea Turner** (66.78) and **Kyle Schwarber** (64.70). Despite both facing Jones, their high efficiency scores are driven by premier lineup positioning and positive park factors, which help mitigate the SP skill metrics. **Lars Nootbaar** also earns a strong recommendation at 65.16; he is our most aggressive play today, as the matchup against Waldrep provides a massive +19% boost from SP skill and a favorable +11% platoon advantage, creating significant upside for power production.\n\nWe continue to prioritize situational efficiency to manage our game caps, which led to a difficult bench decision regarding **Dillon Dingler**. While **Dillon Dingler** (36.36) showed strong underlying metrics including an impressive 0.389 xwOBA, the combination of facing Eovaldi\u2014an elite hold starter who suppresses stolen base attempts\u2014and a negative dynamic platoon split forced him below our 40.0 floor. Elsewhere, **Christian Yelich** (55.42) remains a viable start despite a tough matchup against Burns, as his +13% platoon edge and leadoff status keep him well above the bench threshold. We are also monitoring the status of **Chase Meidroth** (47.29) and **Luke Raley** (52.51); both are marginal starts that require close observation, as their lower batting order slots limit their ceiling in today's 5x5 Roto context. With **Yordan Alvarez**, **Caleb Durbin**, and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** sidelined by team off-days, our bench depth is intentionally thin, focusing our efforts on the high-efficiency potential of our active starters like **Junior Caminero** and **Lars Nootbaar**.",
